yeah we're picking up reports from all  over the city it's crazy up there  crazier than normal you mean yo Mikey  that beats almost warmed up a what come  on  and like we got all stinking night  patients dude wise men say you can't  rush perfection it's a microwave bleep  everyone it's Peter Brown standing in  for freelance reviewer Justin Clark for  his review of Teenage Mutant Ninja  Turtles mutants in Manhattan without a  doubt the new Ninja Turtles game is a  disappointment one multiplied several  times over not just by its pedigree but  by the fact that the right ingredients  for a good game are present bringing the  turtles to life using the same  attractive cel-shaded style as this  simple but satisfying transformers  devastation is the right choice sticking  to the beat'em up genre that has served  the Turtles so well since 1989 is  another good choice the game also  manages to get the tone right with  cutscenes delivering enjoyable slices of  the turtles as wisecracking cartoon  crime fighters trying to figure out  shredder and krang's latest evil plan  and yet the second the controller is in  your hands all the surface details that  the game gets right quickly fall away  the tutorial in the first stage  introduced a plethora of new game  mechanics green spheres can be collected  to gain items and bonuses the parry  system depending on when your turtle  dodges can have three different effects  on an enemy there's a takedown mechanic  where unaware enemies can be destroyed  and one hit if you can stealth close  enough enjutsu special attacks done by  holding a trigger button and pressing  one of the face buttons can be swapped  around in menus and unlocked using XP  during battle  items and stat boosts can also be  purchased from splinter in the turtles  lair whenever you find an open manhole  all of this creates the illusion of an  action RPG with depth but it's a farce  aside from the ninjutsu attacks there  are very few battles in this game that  cannot be solved by spamming basic light  and heavy attacks the rest however are  needless distractions items are all over  the place but only the pizzas which  restore health are of any service in a  major fight ranged weapons don't chip  away nearly as much help as they should  and are awkward to pull out in battle  power boosts offer the same minimal  effect the core of the game is certainly  easy enough to understand with two  attacks to jump in and dodge but hits  have no heft and even the simplest  enemies in the first level taken an  ordinate long  to kill ninjutsu attacks for all their  flash and flair don't always knock an  enemy down only knocking off a small  portion of their health bar the biggest  detriment to combat though is the sheer  inability to tell what exactly is  happening in any of it four turtles  fighting in the same area at the same  time turns the battlefield into a  sparkly cartoon dust cloud of mayhem if  there's a moment when the game actually  starts to come together it's during boss  fights with only one giant enemy to  focus on whose attacks are flashing and  brutal but easy to read the relative  chaos ADIZ encounters can at least make  sense action takes out an arcade  equality that momentarily conjures  memories of great Turtles games from the  past  there are also secret bosses who can  show up when replaying a level and make  a sincere plea for replay value just to  see what twists the game might throw  your way the second time around  however despite its relatively short  playtime you can see Manhattan isn't a  boss rush sort of game there's a lot of  running around see me looking sewers  running across poorly designed rooftops  and performing random disjointed and  boring fetch quests for a woefully  underutilized April O'Neil before it's  time for a big fight and once again the  key problem leaves its head fights are  just wild button mashing affairs where  the fruits of your labors leave you at  effect rather than looking forward to  what Michelangelo could do to the next  guy or what Donatello might pull off  this time  for the first time since the infamous  dam level in the original Turtles game  on the NES god it sucks to be a  turquoise man say  man I'm totally a wise man
 

